            <ArticleTitle>Manual therapy with steroid injections in low-back pain. Improvement of quality of life in a controlled trial with four months' follow-up.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>83-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare prospectively the effect of manual treatment such as manipulation, specific mobilization, muscle stretching, auto-traction, and cortisone injections with standardized conventional but optimized activating treatment by primary health care teams.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective controlled multicentre trial with four months' follow-up.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Kopparberg County, Sweden. Six primary health care or occupational health care centres, representing a catchment area of 56000 residents participated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">101 outpatients with acute or subacute low-back pain were, during the period February 1988 to April 1989, randomly allocated to one of two treatment groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Quality of life was assessed at baseline and at four months by means of visual analogue scales (VAS). The occurrence of 27 different symptoms of a psychosomatic character was surveyed initially and at four months by questions answered by &quot;yes&quot; or &quot;no&quot; in a questionnaire.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were significant differences concerning quality of life and presence of general symptoms in favour of the group receiving manual treatment with steroid injections.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Manual treatment with steroid injections was superior to conventional treatment in minimizing mental and somatic symptoms and increasing quality of life, in parallel with other measures of improvement.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
